    "### Why our Portfolio A strategy makes financial sense\n",
    "\n",
    "We designed Portfolio A for a client willing to accept more risk in pursuit of higher growth. We first remove stocks that do not meet the assignment’s eligibility and other rules that were mentioned. For each remaining stock, we estimate beta from daily CAD returns over the trailing year, comparing the stock with the S&P 500 valued in CAD. Beta measures how sensitive a stock’s past returns have been to market movements. For comparison a beta above 1 has historically moved more than the market, while a beta below 1 has moved less. We combine beta with the previous year’s price return, giving both measures equal importance after scaling them. This favours stocks that have recently performed well and have shown greater market sensitivity, which fits a higher risk objective, although neither measure guarantees future returns.\n",
    "\n",
    "We selected up to five of the highest scoring eligible stocks in Technology and Communication Services for a growth allocation of up to 40%. We select the rest from other sectors. Within those groups, higher scores receive larger starting weights, subject to a 15% limit for any one stock and a 40% limit for any one sector. These limits reduce the chance that one company or sector dominates the portfolio while keeping the growth oriented allocation. We convert USD holdings to CAD for portfolio values and fees  and are using fractional shares to invest close to the budget.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What the evidence and equal-weight comparison show\n",
    "\n",
    "In the course example priced on September 30, 2026, both portfolios held the same 15 stocks. From October 1, 2025 to September 30, 2026, the growth weights returned 42.924%, compared with 39.733% at equal weights. Daily CAD volatility also rose from 1.110% to 1.238%. The growth client earned a higher historical return in exchange for larger daily swings. These are cumulative price returns with fixed shares. Betas and past returns helped choose the stocks, so this sample also reflects how the stocks were selected. The comparison table updates when the inputs change.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What could go wrong\n",
    "\n",
    "Our method can perform poorly if the market turns against higher-beta or recently strong stocks. Beta and past returns describe the period used to calculate them; neither guarantees future performance, and recent winners can reverse. Stocks can also become more correlated during a market decline, so holding several companies does not remove broad market risk. The sector and individual-stock limits reduce concentration, but they do not prevent losses, and the growth allocation can still make the portfolio sensitive to changes in technology valuations.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because we hold Canadian and U.S. stocks, changes in the USD/CAD exchange rate can raise or lower the CAD value of U.S. holdings even when their USD prices do not change. Our historical comparison covers a limited period and ignores dividends as required by the assignment, so it may not represent other market conditions or total investor experience."
