    "Equal-weight comparison explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "We compare our score weighted portfolio with an equal weight portfolio containing exactly the same selected stocks. Both use CAD values and the same historical period. The equal weight portfolio starts at 1/n per stock, while our strategy uses the score based starting weights.\n",
    "\n",
    "Using the November 25–26 sample for the portfolio we got a historical price return of 36.81% for our score-weighted portfolio and 30.37% for the equal weight portfolio. Annualized volatility was 24.69% for our portfolio and 21.25% for equal weighting. This is 6.44 percentage points more historical price return alongside 3.44 percentage points more annualized volatility for the score weighted portfolio. The comparison period was November 25, 2025 to November 25, 2026. Both portfolios use the same selected stocks, CAD prices, and fixed starting shares with no rebalancing  weights drift as prices change. Because our scores use beta and returns measured over this same period, this is a historical illustration of the weighting choice, not an out of sample test or a forecast. We use price returns and exclude dividends where needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "These return figures are annualized average daily returns which is a mean daily return multiplied by 252, not cumulative holding-period returns. The comparison code above now measures cumulative price return using fixed starting shares and lets the weights drift."
   ]
